<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Lifelong sudden spells and near-daily headaches since a severe head injury in early adolescence, in a 75-year-old woman. Please advise whether the spells are focal seizures or sleep attacks, and on headache prevention. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two to three weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Are the spells focal seizures or sleep attacks, and what testing will tell them apart?</item><item>Should antiseizure medicine be started now?</item><item>Do you agree this is chronic post-traumatic headache with medication overuse, and which non-sedating preventive would you use?</item><item>If the spells are sleep attacks, how should her daytime sleepiness be treated without sedating her?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mrs. Tubman is seen at the request of Dr. Ruiz-Delgado two weeks after her primary care visit for headaches on most days and sudden sleep spells since a severe head injury in her early teens. She stopped daily ibuprofen at that visit; her headaches were worse for the first several days and have eased since, and she has used acetaminophen on no more than 2 days a week. The spells continue several times a day, often while seated, and end after a few minutes with full recovery. Witnesses have described no stiffening, jerking, lip-smacking, tongue biting, or incontinence, and she is not confused afterward. She has never had sudden weakness with laughter or strong emotion. She does not drive and does not want medicine that makes her duller. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Harriet Tubman was born into slavery in Dorchester County, Maryland, in early 1822 by the National Park Service account, and was named Araminta, or Minty, by her parents, Ben and Rit Ross; she was the fifth of nine children. [D]</item><item>Her birth year is uncertain: the Dictionary of Canadian Biography gives 1820 or 1821, while the National Park Service and the Maryland State Archives give about 1822. [D]</item><item>Hired out from the age of six, Tubman was struck in the head in her early teens, about age 13 in the mid-1830s, by an iron weight an overseer threw at another enslaved person at a village store; she was nearly killed, received little or no medical care, and was affected by the injury for the rest of her life. [D]</item><item>After the injury Tubman had headaches and sudden spells of sleep that came on even in the middle of work or conversation, at frequent intervals, and ended abruptly after a few minutes, along with vivid dreams and visions; contemporaries described the spells into her old age. [D]</item><item>Historians have proposed that the injury caused temporal lobe epilepsy, citing dreamlike trances in which she appeared conscious. [R]</item><item>A 2023 review argued that temporal lobe epilepsy is unlikely, because she could be roused and no auras, automatisms, or post-ictal confusion were described, and proposed post-traumatic narcolepsy instead, while concluding that no definitive explanation is possible. [R]</item><item>In her later years Tubman said that a Boston surgeon had opened her skull to relieve her headaches; historians place the operation in the late 1890s at Massachusetts General Hospital, but no record of it has been found. [D]</item><item>Tubman married John Tubman in 1844, escaped to Philadelphia in 1849, guided about 70 people to freedom by 1860, served the Union army in South Carolina as a nurse, scout, and spy, and settled in Auburn, New York, where she married Nelson Davis in 1869. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

