<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Recurrent sudden spells since adolescent head injury, cause under evaluation. Brief spells that end abruptly with full recovery and no convulsive or post-ictal features favor sleep attacks, such as narcolepsy after brain injury, over focal seizures. Routine EEG today shows intermittent left frontal slowing over the old injury but no epileptiform discharges, and MRI shows normal hippocampi. A routine EEG cannot exclude focal seizures, so typical spells need to be captured, and daytime sleepiness needs formal sleep testing. [S]</item><item>Chronic post-traumatic headache (G44.329). Headache on most days since the injury, worsened by analgesic overuse, with a normal neurological examination and no new lesion on MRI. Blood pressure 140/84 mmHg. Candesartan is a non-sedating preventive that also lowers her mildly raised blood pressure. [S]</item><item>Medication-overuse headache, withdrawal under way (G44.40). Two weeks without ibuprofen; the expected withdrawal worsening has passed. [S]</item><item>Sequelae of adolescent traumatic brain injury (S06.9XAS). Old depressed left frontal skull defect with small underlying encephalomalacia on MRI, matching the healed depression on examination; no tumor or other lesion. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Today&apos;s routine EEG and brain MRI, her headache diary, and the primary care laboratory results, PROMIS score, headache-day count, and screening scores from two weeks ago. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Ambulatory EEG with video to capture typical spells, booked for early next month. [S]</item><item>Overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test, booked after the ambulatory EEG. [S]</item><item>No antiseizure medicine unless the captured spells prove epileptic. [S]</item><item>If the sleep studies confirm a central disorder of hypersomnolence, start modafinil 100 mg each morning with two scheduled 15-minute naps a day, and check blood pressure and heart rate at follow-up. [S]</item><item>Start candesartan 8 mg daily today for headache prevention. Check potassium and creatinine in four weeks before increasing the dose, and judge the preventive effect at two to three months. [S]</item><item>Acetaminophen on no more than 2 days a week; no ibuprofen or other NSAIDs. Continue the headache diary. [S]</item><item>No further imaging unless new neurological signs appear. [S]</item><item>Sit down at the first sign of sleepiness until the cause of the spells is known; she does not drive. [S]</item><item>Letter to Dr. Ruiz-Delgado today. The plan was discussed with the patient, who agrees with it and with avoiding sedating medicine. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Results visit in four weeks, after the ambulatory EEG and sleep studies, with potassium and creatinine before the candesartan dose is increased. Three-month review of sleepiness and headache days. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
